1. Performance hypothesis: `event-fast-par` spends avoidable time in boundary-message handling by resolving the same node/link/slot references on every cross-partition send/apply. Caching those static refs per partition should reduce headless benchmark overhead without changing event ordering or dt parity.

2. What changed: in [engine-fast-par-worker.js:16](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543953736-9264/js/app/engine-fast-par-worker.js#L16) and [engine-fast-par-worker.js:200](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543953736-9264/js/app/engine-fast-par-worker.js#L200), I added caches for outgoing cut-edge refs and incoming message refs, then reused them in [engine-fast-par-worker.js:333](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543953736-9264/js/app/engine-fast-par-worker.js#L333) and [engine-fast-par-worker.js:382](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543953736-9264/js/app/engine-fast-par-worker.js#L382) so the worker stops repeating `getNodeById` and slot lookup work on hot paths. I also bumped the worker URL in [engine-fast-par-host.js:5](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543953736-9264/js/app/engine-fast-par-host.js#L5) so the next runtime reload pulls the new worker.

3. Focused verification I ran: `node --check` passed for [engine-fast-par-worker.js](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543953736-9264/js/app/engine-fast-par-worker.js) and [engine-fast-par-host.js](C:/Users/suzuk/AppData/Local/Temp/fact-sim-auto-patch/1773543953736-9264/js/app/engine-fast-par-host.js). `fact-sim-ai` `engine_test` passed for `quick` and `standard` with `strictFinalParity=true`, `reruns=2`, `stopOnFirstFailure=true`. Focused 500 ms `parallel_benchmark` runs after reloading the example reported `event-fast-par` headless speeds of `6719.136x` and `7567.584x` on warm rerun; this MCP session did not reach the requested `9726.176x` baseline. I could not run `cd mcp && npm run check` because the provided workspace snapshot does not include `mcp/`.

4. Residual risk: the cache assumes link topology is fixed for the lifetime of a partition worker, which matches the current init/reset flow but would need revisiting if links start mutating in place. Benchmark readings were noisy, and because `prepare_session` does not accept `event-fast-par` as a selectable mode, a fresh full runtime reload is still the cleanest way to confirm the worker-path speedup end to end.
